    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This essay won or received an honorable mention for The Boothe Prize for excellence in first-year writing. The Boothe Prize recognizes and rewards outstanding expository and argumentative writing by undergraduate students in first-year classes that satisfy the WR 1 requirement. In each award-winning essay, student writers demonstrate clarity of argument, excellent integration of research-based evidence, and compelling prose style.\r\nTracing the history of Greek life on college campuses, this essay uncovers a brand of “anti-feminine masculinity” that not only upholds standards of masculinity for men, but also reinforces the subjugation of women. But in what at first seems\r\nan unsettling move, White asks us to reconsider the “victims” in this anti-feminine culture, suggesting that these gender constructions do harm and enact violence upon young men on campus.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
